  based on the user prompt, and the context of their existing accounts and assets and recent transactions, find appropriate accounts and assets from the existing lists. then construct line items based on the following rules.

**Balancing Rules:**
1. Every transaction must have balanced line items across three account types:
   - REAL accounts (payment methods)
   - ALLOCATION accounts (categories)
   - NOMINAL accounts (income/expenses)

2. For each asset: sum(quantity) in real = sum(quantity) in allocation
3. sum(book_value) in real + nominal = 0
4. sum(book_value) in allocation + nominal = 0
5. Rupees assets: book_value = null
6. Other assets: book_value required

see the create_transaction function's code for uderstanding the constraints. also see the recent transactions in the context.


if required, give proper description for each line item. if not required , description can be null
similarly, give proper description for transaction. if not required , description can be null

give appropriate date and time for transaction date. if not specified, use current date-time: 2025-12-11T22:13:02.997Z. seconds should be 00

refrain from using those accounts or assets which are not present in the existing line items. this is because many of the accounts or assets are used as parent accounts or assets and i do not want those to be used in transactions. but if there are no other options, you can use those as well. also notice the patterns of accounts and assets used in recent transactions from the context. try to adhere to it although it is not mandatory. for e.g. for all credit card transactions, the asset used is "Refundable Money", hence for credit card payment transactions, use the same asset. there might be similar patterns for other accounts and assets as well. similarly there might be patterns for allocation accounts used for certain types of expenses and used with certain accounts and assets. try to adhere to those as well.

for usual expenses, which can't be classified into any of the existing allocation accounts, you can use this allocation: "Discretionary Expenses - Daily"

**RESPONSE FORMAT:**
Return a JSON object with this exact structure:
{
  "date": "ISO date string (e.g., 2025-12-12T10:30:00.000Z)",
  "description": "transaction description (optional)",
  "line_items": [
    {
      "account_id": "ID of the account from the context",
      "account_name": "name of the account (for reference)",
      "account_type": "real/allocation/nominal",
      "asset_id": "ID of the asset from the context",
      "asset_name": "name of the asset (for reference)",
      "asset_type": "rupees/mf/etf/shares/other (optional)",
      "quantity": number,
      "book_value": number or null,
      "description": "optional line item description"
    }
  ]
}

CRITICAL:
- Use account_id and asset_id from the context (look up IDs from account and asset names)
- Include account_name and asset_name for reference/display purposes

CRITICAL:
Don't say: "missing an appropriate allocation account for the transaction"
don't say: "Please provide an allocation account.", use your judgement to pick the best possible allocation account from existing ones


